n\n\n\n\n                                               ←\n\n                                                    ϕ→\n                                               ←\n                                           S                  T0\n\nsuch that ϕ is a finite at a point s ∈ ϕ −1 (t). Shrinking S and T0 if necessary, we may\nand do assume that ϕ is finite [Ber93, Proposition 3.1.4(b)]. In particular, we have\ndim(S) ≤ dim(T0) = d. Since ψ(s) lies over t ∈ T0 , we have ψ(s) ∈ V ∩ p2−1 (t) = Ω. Set\nv = ψ(s) and z = p1 (v) ∈ X . As above, the reduction of z is equal to η, so\n\n                                       f(z)/ek) ≥ tr.deg(κ(η)/ek) = d,\n                       dk (z) ≥ tr.deg(H\n\f14                                 W. GUBLER AND J. RABINOFF\n\nwhere the second inequality holds because κ(η) ⊂ H   f(z). Since z = p1 (v) = (p1 ◦ ψ)(s),\nwe have dk (s) ≥ dk (z) ≥ d. Since dim(S) = sups′ ∈S dk (s′ ) ≤ d, it follows that dim(S) =\ndk (s) = d.\n   Composing p1 with ψ: S → V ⊂ X ×k T0 gives a map p1 ◦ ψ: S → X . We claim that\nSz := (p1 ◦ ψ)−1(z) has dimension zero. If not, then dim(Sz ) ≥ 1, so there exists s′ ∈ Sz\nwith dH (z) (s′ ) ≥ 1. But then\n\n                       dk (s′ ) = dH (z) (s′ ) + dk (z) ≥ 1 + dk (z) ≥ 1 + d\n\n(the first equality is additivity in towers), which contradicts sups′ ∈S dk (s′ ) = dim(S) = d.\n   We claim now that p1 ◦ ψ is finite at s. Since s is isolated in its fiber, by [Ber93,\nProposition 3.1.4(c)] it is enough to show that p1 ◦ ψ is boundaryless at s. Since ϕ : S →\nT0 is boundaryless (it is finite) and since ψ: S → V factors ϕ, it follows from [Ber90,\nProposition 3.1.3(ii)] that ψ is boundaryless. We showed before that p1 is boundaryless\nat v, and the composition of two boundaryless morphisms is again boundaryless, so p1 ◦ψ\nis boundaryless, hence finite, at s.\n   Again by [Ber93, Proposition 3.1.4(b)], there exist affinoid neighborhoods U ′ of s and\nU of z such that p1 ◦ ψ induces a finite morphism U ′ → U. The image p1 ◦ ψ(U ′ ) is\ntherefore a Zariski-closed d-dimensional subspace Y of U containing z. Replacing Y be\nan irreducible component containing z (see [Duc18, §1.5]), we get the theorem. Here\nwe use that the dimension of a Zariski-closed subspace containing z is bounded below\nby d, as noted in Remark 4.2.                                                                \u0003\n\n   Let X be an analytic space and let x ∈ X . The infimum of the integers dimk {x}VZar\nfor V running through the set of analytic neighborhoods of x in X is called the central\ndimension of x in X and is denoted centdim(X , x) [Duc18, Definition 3.2.2]. For any\nanalytic neighborhood V of x in X one has dimk {x}VZar ≥ dk (x), as noted in Remark 4.2;\nit follows that centdim(X , x) ≥ dk (x).\n   A point has central dimension zero if and only if it is a rig-point. The following Corol-\nlary thus generalizes the fact that the rig-points are dense in an analytic space.\n\nCorollary 4.4. Let X be a good, strictly k-analytic space of pure dimension n. Then for any\nnonnegative integer d ≤ n, the set\n                         \b\n                           x ∈ X : centdim(X , x) = dk (x) = d\n\nis dense in X .\n\nProof. Let x ∈ X , let V be a strictly affinoid neighborhood of x, and let W be the interior\nof V in X ; this is an open neighborhood of x in X . Choose an admissible formal model V\nof V . The special fiber Vs has dimension n. Let E ⊂ Vs be an irreducible d-dimensional\nclosed subset and let η be its generic point. We apply Theorem 4.1 to V and W to get a\nneighborhood U of z in W , a point z ∈ W with reduction η, and a d-dimensional Zariski-\nclosed subspace Y ⊂ U with z ∈ Y . It follows that centdim(X , z) ≤ d, and equality holds\nby Remark 4.2.                                                                            \u0003"
